  <section class="section wrap service-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Start with the frame</h2>
    <p>
      Not every chair should be reupholstered, and saying so out loud is part of the job. The frame
      decides it. Hardwood, joined and doweled instead of stapled, will take new fabric almost
      regardless of age, and eight-way hand-tied springs are worth keeping. Good bones under an
      unfashionable fabric is the ideal candidate. A failing frame on a particleboard base usually
      is not. A loose joint can be reglued; a cracked rail is a different conversation, and that is
      better known before anything comes apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sentiment counts, and it counts legitimately. A chair from a grandparent's house gets done
      properly because it is that chair, and no other reason is required. That decision belongs to
      the client, made with clear information rather than being talked into or out of it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Fabric that survives the room</h2>
    <p>
      Upholstery fabric answers to different demands than drapery. It gets sat on, leaned against,
      spilled on, and cleaned. Double rubs measure abrasion resistance, and the number that suits a
      formal dining chair is nowhere near what a family room sectional needs. Performance fabrics
      have improved enough to choose on their own merits now, not as a concession to children and
      dogs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pattern scale matters more here than almost anywhere, because the fabric is being fitted to a
      three-dimensional object. A large repeat can be beautiful on a sofa back and unusable on a
      tight-seat chair. Railroading, whether a pattern runs along the bolt or across it, decides
      whether a wide piece can be covered without a seam down the middle. All of that is settled
      before yardage is ordered.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">The details that show</h2>
    <p>
      Welt does more to the character of a finished piece than most people expect. Self-welt keeps
      it quiet. Contrast welt gives it an edge and draws the shape out. A tailored skirt reads
      nothing like a waterfall, and an exposed leg reads differently again. Cushion fill decides
      how the piece feels a year in: down-wrapped foam where softness matters, something firmer
      where people sit every single day.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pattern placement is where the care becomes visible. A motif centered on the seat and
      centered again on the back. Stripes running true down an arm. These are decisions made by
      someone standing over the piece, not by a machine following a cutting order.
    </p>
  </section>

